Release the GIL while a statement is reset or finalized.

A statement that stopped at a row has not halted yet. Its reset halts
it, and in autocommit mode the halt commits, which can fsync. apsw
called sqlite3_reset and sqlite3_finalize with the GIL held, so a
cursor dropped at a row froze every Python thread for the commit: 22s
on btrfs, measured. Lillecarl/pymux#940, Lillecarl/apsw#3.

Every callback that a halt can reach takes the GIL itself, as it does
for sqlite3_step in cursor.c.

--- a/src/statementcache.c
+++ b/src/statementcache.c
@@ -91,7 +91,12 @@

   Py_CLEAR(s->query);

-  res = s->vdbestatement ? sqlite3_finalize(s->vdbestatement) : SQLITE_OK;
+  res = SQLITE_OK;
+  if (s->vdbestatement)
+  {
+    Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS res = sqlite3_finalize(s->vdbestatement);
+    Py_END_ALLOW_THREADS;
+  }

 #if SC_STATEMENT_RECYCLE_BIN_ENTRIES > 0
   if (sc && sc->recycle_bin_next < SC_STATEMENT_RECYCLE_BIN_ENTRIES)
@@ -125,7 +130,8 @@
   {
     APSWStatement *evictee = NULL;

-    res = sqlite3_reset(statement->vdbestatement);
+    Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS res = sqlite3_reset(statement->vdbestatement);
+    Py_END_ALLOW_THREADS;

